# Context

## Why This Way
The owner's policy: every build landed after fixes or features
carries a bumped version. Deriving the stamp at build time in
scripts/build-ldflags.sh (the single ldflags choke point for both
install.sh and the Makefile) enforces it automatically - no one
has to remember to bump VERSION between releases.

The count is commits since the last VERSION-touching commit, not
since a tag: releases 0.8.0-0.8.2 and 0.9.0 were left untagged by
choice, so tags are unreliable here. Every post-bump commit bumps
the patch; classifying commits as fix vs feat is not possible
because 79 of the last 100 subjects carry no prefix.

## Gotchas
- Explicit version arguments pin the stamp verbatim (Formula,
  make build VERSION=x, test fixtures). An empty argument derives.
- A dirty VERSION file derives from the dirty content; the
  "(modified)" marker disambiguates.
- git log -1 -- VERSION only walks history reachable from HEAD, so
  the bump commit is always HEAD or an ancestor; the count cannot
  fail on an old checkout.

